facts:
For $k > 0$, $|ax + b| < k$ means $-k < ax + b < k$, and $|ax + b| \le k$ means $-k \le ax + b \le k$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-4.4,0) -- (4.4,0);
\node[right] at (4.5,0) {$X$};
\draw[very thick] (-2.4,0) -- (2.4,0);
\draw[fill=white] (-2.4,0) circle (3pt);
\draw[fill=white] (2.4,0) circle (3pt);
\node[below] at (-2.4,-0.25) {$-k$};
\node[below] at (0,-0.25) {$0$};
\node[below] at (2.4,-0.25) {$k$};
\node at (0,1.0) {$|X| < k$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For $k > 0$, $|ax + b| > k$ means $ax + b < -k$ or $ax + b > k$, with $\ge$ giving the same two pieces closed.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-4.4,0) -- (4.4,0);
\node[right] at (4.5,0) {$X$};
\draw[very thick, <-] (-3.9,0) -- (-2.4,0);
\draw[very thick, ->] (2.4,0) -- (3.9,0);
\draw[fill=white] (-2.4,0) circle (3pt);
\draw[fill=white] (2.4,0) circle (3pt);
\node[below] at (-2.4,-0.25) {$-k$};
\node[below] at (0,-0.25) {$0$};
\node[below] at (2.4,-0.25) {$k$};
\node at (0,1.0) {$|X| > k$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
